<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Bounded Reach Contract ~ a signpost leased without leasing the self

''PROPOSED — design intent with an authored consistency control; no DNS provider, ACME issuance path,
or deployment route stands built.'' This meme gives the local operator's "DNS token" a precise name:
the ''bounded reach grant''. It is a bilateral, scoped capability contract, never a bearer token that
floats free of its holder, subtree, Nexus charter epoch, or ending.

The contract lets an operator make a local LAN service discoverable through a memorable DreamNet name
and earn a browser-trusted origin certificate for that bounded subtree. A name remains rendezvous only;
the operator's self-certifying key remains identity; a separate membership or content capability says
what peers may join or read. A local resolver may answer a local question without making that answer a
global fact.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/capability-matrix>>

!! Five capabilities, five holders

|!capability |!holder |!scope |!permits |!does not permit |
|Nexus carriage contract |two sovereign operators |one agreed charter epoch and transit relation |blind relay carriage under the contract |DNS writes, identity, membership, or content read |
|LAN resolver policy |the local operator |one causal LAN and its resolver view |name → private address for local clients |public DNS answers, ACME authorization, or a global roster |
|bounded origin-attestation grant |Nexus/Kahu plus the contracted operator |one operator subtree and one charter epoch |the named DNS-01/account-bound issuance and renewal acts, if the chosen CA supports them |apex write, another operator's subtree, peer identity, membership, or content read |
|self-certifying node/document identity |the vessel or operator |the key and its signed delegation graph |prove continuity and sign authored facts |a memorable DNS name, LAN address, or certificate issuer deciding standing |
|Onomancy DNSSEC binding |DNSSEC zone plus the document's binding record |one public `@name` |point a memorable name at an existing self-certifying document |TLS issuance, LAN resolution, membership, content read, or identity creation |

''A public name grants RENDEZVOUS only.'' The public authoritative view names where a traveler may
knock. It never names who the traveler is, who belongs to the Nexus, or what bytes the traveler may
read. A browser's origin attestation proves the serving origin to the browser; it does not prove the
peer behind the origin.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/grant-shape>>

!! The grant carries bounded evidence

The contract's signed or otherwise jointly witnessed record SHOULD carry these fields. A future
implementation MAY encode them differently, but it MUST preserve their meanings and boundaries:

```text
subject         = operator's public ACME-account identity
nexus           = one Nexus charter epoch link
subtree         = one bounded public hostname subtree
actions         = DNS-01 / account-bound issuance / renewal only
origin-scope    = one narrow wildcard or equivalent subtree scope
publication     = public DNS evidence only; no public operator roster
ending          = non-renewal or explicit causal withdrawal
identity        = external to the grant; node/document keys remain sovereign
```

The Kahu or Nexus governance hand performs the rare admission and withdrawal act. The operator holds
their ACME account key, certificate private key, and ordinary renewal path. A quorum belongs on the rare
grant act, never on every frequent renewal. The grant blesses a record or bounded challenge relation,
never a certificate held by the Kahu.

The word ''token'' therefore names an operator-facing metaphor only. The protocol object reads as a
scoped contract whose holder, action, subtree, epoch, and ending remain inspectable. It MUST NOT be
serializable into an unscoped bearer secret.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/lifecycle>>

!! Lifecycle ~ contract first, renewal local, withdrawal severable

# ''Propose.'' The operator and Nexus name the subject, subtree, charter epoch, permitted action set,
  local resolver responsibility, and withdrawal/expiry behavior. The proposal says which party holds
  each DNS record and which CA behavior remains an unresolved fork.
# ''Admit.'' The rare Kahu act publishes or authorizes only the bounded evidence required by the chosen
  challenge path. It does not copy the operator's private key, create a roster, or mint the operator's
  node identity.
# ''Serve.'' The local recursive resolver may answer the operator's LAN with a private address while
  the public authoritative view answers the general internet consistently and unfiltered. The same
  hostname can therefore resolve differently by causal island without either answer becoming universal.
# ''Renew.'' The operator's own account and key perform ordinary renewal. A certificate's validity is a
  named Web2 bridge and a local witness; it does not order CRDT facts, decide membership, or settle an
  identity dispute. No global wall-clock fact enters mesh ordering.
# ''Withdraw.'' The rare authority removes or declines the bounded evidence. Future issuance or renewal
  fails after the relevant record disappears; already-issued certificates retain their ordinary validity
  window. Keys, documents, peer relations, and local stories remain with the operator.
# ''Exit.'' The operator can leave the Nexus, move the name to another reach shore, or stand a Nexus of
  their own while retaining the self-certifying document and its signed continuity. A lost signpost is
  a reach loss, never a self loss.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/dns-boundaries>>

!! Two DNS views and one named governance fork

''Split horizon belongs to the local recursive resolver.'' A LAN resolver may return a private address
for its own clients. The public authoritative DNS view MUST answer the whole internet consistently and
remain available to multi-perspective validation and DNSSEC validation. Assigning split horizon to the
authoritative view would make DNS-01 nondeterministic and would turn a local causal answer into a false
global claim.

The current topology says both that an operator receives a delegated `_acme-challenge` label and that
the civic Kahu holds the contracted operator's DNS records in trust. Those statements can describe two
different record classes, but they do not yet settle the production contract. The following fork stays
OPEN and MUST receive a provider-specific, governance-approved ruling before deployment work:

* ''Fork A — challenge delegation.'' The Kahu retains the public authoritative zone and delegates only
  the challenge evidence for a bounded subtree. The operator owns the ACME account and service key;
  the chosen CA must support the account-bound challenge shape without granting apex or sibling scope.
* ''Fork B — Kahu-published evidence.'' The operator supplies signed or account-bound challenge data,
  while the Kahu publishes it in trust. This preserves zone custody but adds a recurring Kahu service
  obligation and needs a precise proof that the Kahu cannot impersonate the operator's account.

CAA does not silently resolve the fork. RFC 8659's first CAA answer replaces the parent set, and the
wildcard lookup strips `*.` before climbing. A shared wildcard and per-operator account pinning can
therefore collide; a delegated operator cannot simply publish the apex holder's CAA policy. The design
must choose the exact CA/account/CAA combination—or explicitly hold CAA as an admission constraint—after
the provider behavior and custody responsibilities receive a separate red-first measurement.

''No implementation claim follows.'' `dns-account-01`, `dns-persist-01`, ACME Parent/Subordinate
Account language, and any provider-specific API remain research inputs until a named provider and
governance hand settle this fork. No code may infer that a `_acme-challenge` delegation grants CAA,
apex write, identity, membership, or read capability.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/evidence-boundaries>>

!! Evidence says what was witnessed

|!evidence |!it can establish |!it cannot establish |
|local resolver answer |this LAN's current name → address view |a public DNS truth, identity, or global reachability |
|public authoritative DNS + DNSSEC |public name/record integrity during its validation window |peer identity, membership, content read, or permanent availability |
|ACME/TLS certificate |a CA-validated origin for a hostname during certificate validity |the operator's node key, document identity, membership, or content authority |
|Onomancy `_onomancy.<name>` binding |a DNSSEC-attested pointer to an existing document key |a certificate, an A/AAAA answer, a LAN route, or a new identity |
|operator key/document signature |continuity of the self-certifying document and its delegation graph |public reachability, CA trust, or Nexus membership by itself |
|carriage contract |the named transit relation and its blind-carriage scope |a right to inspect, alter, or join the carried content |

The evidence remains local and composable. A verifier MAY hold all six readings at once; it MUST retain
their provenance and MUST surface disagreement rather than merge the readings into an authority the
individual evidence never carried.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/gaia-story>>

!! Operators on Gaia (Earth circa 2026) SHALL need

Operators on Gaia (Earth circa 2026) SHALL need to place a Lararium on a household LAN, give its family
devices a memorable DreamNet signpost, and keep the household able to work when the uplink fails. Success
means the local recursive resolver still returns the private service address, the browser continues to
serve the last valid HTTPS origin, the vessel's self-certifying key and local documents remain available,
and no remote DNS response is required for local work. Failure means a public DNS outage is treated as
the loss of the household's identity, or a browser is forced to wait on a cloud resolver before it can
open its own local story.

Operators SHALL need to withdraw or leave a contracted subtree without losing self. Success means the
next renewal or admission attempt no longer receives the withdrawn evidence, while existing documents,
keys, local projections, and peer relations remain inspectable and portable. Failure means withdrawal
deletes a key, strands local content, or leaves a stale public roster asserting that the operator still
belongs. The household SHALL need no public list of every operator: a verifier sees only the DNS evidence
needed for the bounded name. A name SHALL never itself claim identity; the operator's node/document key
answers who, and the DNS name answers where.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/elyncia-story>>

!! Operators on Elyncia (Orichalcum Mythpunk RPG) SHALL need

Operators on Elyncia (Orichalcum Mythpunk RPG) SHALL need to tend a shrine, crossroads, or traveling
Lararium whose DreamNet signpost can disappear while the shrine's own mythic self persists. Success means
a Lamplighter can guide a local party through a darkened uplink by the shrine's local resolver, the shrine
continues to feed its isolated TW5 islands and render its known stories, and a traveler can later verify
the same document key after the public signpost returns or moves. Failure means the Kahu's name becomes the
shrine's soul, a fallen crossroads erases the shrine's history, or an outside DNS answer pretends to know
which spirits belong within its walls.

Operators SHALL need to contract a bounded reach through a Nexus without turning the mythic road into a
registry of souls. Success means a Kahu can admit or withdraw one named road and its origin attestation,
while the shrine's sealed stories, self-certifying document, local companions, and bilateral carriage
compacts survive exit. A public name remains a waystone; Onomancy may point that waystone at an existing
document, while TLS may reassure a browser that it reached the serving origin. Neither names a spirit,
grants membership, or opens a story's read gate. Failure means a waystone seizure changes a character's
identity, a resolver's answer creates a membership claim, or a certificate's expiry reorders the shrine's
causal history.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/consistency-control>>

!! RED-first authored invariant

The bounded-reach control below is a small declaration seam, not a second memetic parser. It reads only
this meme's named `toml bounded-reach-control` block and checks the authored capability values. The
positive control proves that the document keeps DNS rendezvous, local split horizon, public authoritative
DNS, Onomancy, and TLS/ACME in distinct roles. Its weakened fixtures deliberately replace the local
resolver, name, or Onomancy/TLS value and MUST red. The seam therefore checks the invariant the design
claims, rather than counting suggestive words across unrelated prose.

```toml bounded-reach-control
name-grant           = "rendezvous-only"
split-horizon-owner  = "local-recursive-resolver"
authoritative-view   = "public-global-unfiltered"
identity-source      = "self-certifying-key"
membership-source    = "explicit-capability-contract"
read-source          = "explicit-capability-contract"
onomancy-role        = "dnssec-binding-to-existing-document"
tls-acme-role        = "origin-attestation-only"
```

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/held>>

!! Unbuilt holds

This wave lands design and consistency evidence only. DNS provider selection, ACME issuance, CAA
publication, resolver deployment, certificate-expiry implementation, public roster policy, and origin
continuity tests remain held. The ACME/CAA custody fork above must receive an operator-visible ruling
before any deployment path opens.

<<~/ahu>>

